# RSVP -> Google Sheets (server-only; read by the API routes under app/api/).
# Copy to .env.local for local dev; set the same three in Vercel project env vars.
#
# The spreadsheet needs two tabs:
#   Guests    (you fill): party_label | first_name | last_name | display_name | plus_one_allowed | is_kid
#                         | party_id | guest_id   (both optional — see below)
#   Responses (app writes): Timestamp | Name | Email | Yes/No? | Dietary Needs | Additional Messages | Notes
#
# party_id: only needed when TWO SEPARATE HOUSEHOLDS share one party_label (two unrelated
# "The Diaz Family" entries). Give each household its own value — "1" and "2" is fine — on
# EVERY row of BOTH households. A blank cell falls back to the party_label, so a half-filled
# column silently merges the two families into one party, letting either one see and overwrite
# the other's RSVP. The value is scoped to its party_label, so the same "1"/"2" can be reused
# for a different label without colliding. guest_id likewise overrides one person's derived id;
# you shouldn't normally need it. Both are ignored when blank.
#
# Columns are matched by header NAME, so extra/legacy columns and column order are fine — but
# the Responses tab's header row must literally read exactly those 7 labels (case/punctuation as
# shown) for the app to read prior responses back (edit prefill + resubmission detection).
# A plus-one has no column of their own to link them to their host: their Name cell is written as
# "<their name> — guest of <host's name>", and read back the same way.
#
# Leave all three unset (or set RSVP_FIXTURE=1) to run the RSVP flow against an
# in-repo sample list without touching the real sheet.

# Service account address (the "client_email" from the downloaded JSON key).
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=your-sa@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com

# The "private_key" from that JSON key, in quotes. Newlines may be literal \n
# (as Vercel stores them) or real — the route handles both.
GOOGLE_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"

# The long token in the target spreadsheet's URL:
# https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/<THIS_PART>/edit
GOOGLE_SHEET_ID=the-long-id-from-the-sheet-url

# Signs the cookie that proves a browser submitted a given party's RSVP. The
# lookup endpoint is public and matches guests by name, so prior answers (who's
# coming, dietary notes, the email, the message) are returned ONLY to a browser
# holding that cookie — everyone else who knows the name sees just a date.
#
# Any long random string; generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# If UNSET, no cookie is issued and no guest ever gets their answers prefilled —
# they simply fill the form in again. That's the safe direction to fail, so an
# absent value degrades the experience rather than leaking anything.
RSVP_COOKIE_SECRET=

# --- Italy wedding RSVP (/italy-wedding/rsvp) --------------------------------
# A SEPARATE spreadsheet with the same two tabs and headers as above (Guests +
# Responses). Share it with the same service account (Editor). Until this is
# set, the Italy RSVP runs against the in-repo sample list in
# lib/guests-fixture-italy.ts and writes nothing. Deadline: ITALY_RSVP_DEADLINE
# in lib/config.ts.
#
# Step-by-step setup (sheet, tabs, headers, sharing): docs/italy-rsvp-setup.md
#
# Note RSVP_COOKIE_SECRET above does double duty for Italy: the accommodations
# page at /italy-wedding/accommodations is unlocked by the same signed cookie
# that proves this browser submitted an RSVP. Unset it and no guest can reach
# their accommodation details.
ITALY_GOOGLE_SHEET_ID=

# --- Password gate (the bare domain) -----------------------------------------
# Both weddings sit behind one password field at "/". Each password unlocks its
# own section and leaves any grant already held intact, so someone invited to
# both can enter both words over time. Nothing in the UI reveals that a second
# password exists.
#
# Use a long, multi-word passphrase. The brute-force limiter is per-instance
# and best-effort on serverless, so passphrase length is the real protection.
# These are a privacy measure, not security: they keep the site off the open
# web, they do not protect anything that would be damaging to leak.
GATE_PASSWORD_ITALY=
GATE_PASSWORD_CHURCH=

# Signs the cookie recording which sections this browser has unlocked. Any long
# random string; generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# If UNSET, no grant can be issued and NOBODY gets into either site — unlike
# RSVP_COOKIE_SECRET, this one fails closed in a way guests will notice.
SITE_GATE_SECRET=
